  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 322 AM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nEarly this morning, a weak mid/upper trough is sinking southward \ninto the Ozarks while modest mid/upper ridging slides eastward into \nthe central plains. At the surface, a weak ridge is translating \nsoutheastward across the Missouri River Valley. Meanwhile, shallow \nmoisture is plunging south and west into much cooler air \nsupporting expansive low stratus along with areas of drizzle \nand fog impacting much of the region. After sunrise, much of the\nfog, drizzle, and low clouds are forecast to gradually mix out \ndespite an increase in mid to upper level cloud cover limiting \ndiurnal heating. The general expectation is for afternoon highs \nto climb into the 50s, but there is a possibility where portions\nof northern and northeast Kansas struggle to hit 50 should \ncloud cover remain stubborn throughout the day. Cooler air will \nsink into the forecast area tonight, and lows will generally \ndrop into the 30s. Given lingering moisture, most locations \ntonight into Saturday morning should experience widespread frost\nwhich may damage sensitive vegetation.\n\nModest mid/upper ridging and weak southerly flow at the surface will \nsupport a gradual warm up heading into the weekend and early next \nweek. Afternoon highs Saturday through Tuesday will rise into the \n60s and low 70s, and overnight lows will generally bottom out into \nthe upper 30s and 40s. A very weak frontal boundary is currently \nforecast to pass through the region on Tuesday, but this will do \nlittle to impact temperatures. In fact, going into the latter \nportions of next week, the current forecast calls for afternoon \nhighs to remain in the 60s and low 70s, and overnight lows generally \nin the 40s. In the wake of the this frontal passage, there are some \nindications of perhaps a few isolated showers early on Wednesday, \nbut chances remain quite low at this time.",
